INSERT INTO "Article" ("id", "slug", "category", "country", "titleEn", "titleNe", "excerptEn", "excerptNe", "bodyEn", "bodyNe", "sources", "publishedAt", "createdAt", "updatedAt") VALUES
(gen_random_uuid(), 'tnpsc-group-ii-iia-prelims-1-november-20261028', 'global', 'India',
'TNPSC Group II Prelims on 1 November 2026: 4.89 Lakh Candidates Compete for 846 Posts',
'',
'Nearly 4.89 lakh candidates will sit the TNPSC Group II prelims on 1 November 2026 for 846 government posts across Tamil Nadu, with applications already closed on 9 September 2026.',
'',
$$<p>Nearly 4.9 lakh candidates will sit the TNPSC Group II prelims on 1 November 2026, competing for 846 government posts across Tamil Nadu. The TNPSC Group II prelims, covering Group II and Group IIA services, will be held at 1,647 centres across the state, IANS reported from Chennai on 28 October 2026. Applications for this recruitment closed on 9 September 2026, so this is not a fresh application window: the story now is the examination itself, and the scale of competition facing candidates who already applied.</p>
<table><tr><th>At a glance: TNPSC Group II prelims 2026</th><th>Detail</th></tr><tr><td>Exam</td><td>TNPSC Group II and Group IIA preliminary examination, Combined Civil Services Examination-II</td></tr><tr><td>Exam date</td><td>1 November 2026, forenoon session</td></tr><tr><td>Candidates scheduled</td><td>4,89,885</td></tr><tr><td>Total vacancies</td><td>846</td></tr><tr><td>Group II vacancies</td><td>43</td></tr><tr><td>Group IIA vacancies</td><td>803</td></tr><tr><td>Examination centres</td><td>1,647 across Tamil Nadu</td></tr><tr><td>Chennai candidates</td><td>51,721 across 173 centres</td></tr><tr><td>Notification</td><td>No. 07/2026, issued 11 August 2026</td></tr><tr><td>Applications closed</td><td>9 September 2026</td></tr><tr><td>Hall tickets</td><td>Already released; download through the One-Time Registration dashboard</td></tr><tr><td>Selection stages</td><td>Preliminary examination followed by the main examination</td></tr><tr><td>Official websites</td><td>tnpsc.gov.in and tnpscexams.in</td></tr></table>
<h2>TNPSC Group II prelims: 579 candidates for every vacancy</h2>
<p>The headline figure is the competition ratio. IANS reported that 4,89,885 candidates are scheduled to take the examination, which translates into approximately 579 candidates for every notified vacancy. That ratio is what makes the preliminary stage so unforgiving: it is a screening test first, and most candidates will not reach the main examination, however well prepared they feel.</p>
<p>The candidate pool itself is large and varied. IANS reported that 1,84,131 of the scheduled candidates are men, while women constitute more than three-fifths of the total, and 35 transgender persons are also appearing. Chennai alone accounts for 51,721 candidates across 173 centres, slightly more than a tenth of the statewide total, which means the capital's centres will be among the busiest on the day. The remaining candidates are spread across the other centres in Tamil Nadu, 1,647 in total.</p>
<h2>TNPSC Group II prelims posts: what the 846 vacancies cover</h2>
<p>The 846 posts form the vacancy pool for both categories together. DT Next reported that the number was subsequently increased to 846, comprising 43 vacancies in Group II posts and 803 in Group IIA posts. The notified positions include Assistant Inspector and Probation Officer, besides Tamil Nadu Secretariat Assistant and Trade Union Supervisor posts, IANS reported. The recruitment notification, No. 07/2026, was issued on 11 August 2026, and online applications were accepted until 9 September 2026.</p>
<p>The preliminary examination will serve as the initial screening stage, IANS reported. The selection process will be conducted in two stages, preliminary and main examinations, DT Next reported. The date of the main examination has not been stated in the reports read for this article, so candidates should watch the commission's official websites for that announcement rather than relying on dates circulating on social media.</p>
<h2>What candidates should do before the TNPSC Group II prelims</h2>
<p>Hall tickets have already been released for candidates who applied for the examination, IANS reported. Candidates permitted to appear can download their hall tickets from the commission's official websites, tnpsc.gov.in and tnpscexams.in. According to a TNPSC notification, candidates must log in through their One-Time Registration dashboard and enter their application number and date of birth to download the hall ticket, DT Next reported.</p>
<p>The checks before 1 November 2026 are practical, and they should be done early. Candidates have been advised to read and follow the instructions printed on their hall tickets, and to verify their personal details, examination date and time, venue and address in advance, DT Next reported. They must also ensure that they do not carry any unauthorised items into the examination hall and that they comply with the commission's guidelines. A wrong venue, a misspelt name left unchecked, or a banned item carried by habit can each cost a candidate the attempt, regardless of preparation.</p>
<p>For the examination itself, the preliminary test is scheduled for the forenoon session on 1 November 2026. Candidates should plan travel to the centre with time to spare, since 1,647 centres across the state will all be handling large numbers at the same time, and Chennai's 173 centres alone will take in more than 51,000 candidates.</p>
<h2>Why the TNPSC Group II prelims matter beyond the numbers</h2>
<p>Group II and IIA posts sit in the everyday machinery of state government, from secretariat assistance to probation and inspection work. The posts on offer are therefore not abstract: they are the roles that handle files, field visits, supervision and public-facing administration across Tamil Nadu's departments. That is also why the competition is so heavy, and why a single preliminary paper carries so much weight for nearly five lakh applicants.</p>
<p>The timing matters too. With the examination only days away, the useful work now is revision, hall ticket checks and logistics, not fresh applications or new paperwork. Candidates who applied before 9 September 2026 are the only ones in this round, and anyone who missed that window will need to wait for a future notification rather than trying to join this examination.</p>
<h2>A note for readers in Nepal</h2>
<p>For readers in Nepal, the position needs to be plain. This is a Tamil Nadu state government examination, and applications closed on 9 September 2026, so it is not an open vacancy for new applicants from Nepal or anywhere else. The hall ticket download applies only to candidates who already applied. No agent can add a name to the examination list now, and anyone promising a seat or a hall ticket for payment should not be trusted. The only reliable sources are tnpsc.gov.in and tnpscexams.in.</p>
<p>Growentix also covers other Tamil Nadu public service recruitment, including <a href="https://growentix.cloud/articles/tnpsc-group-4-6574-posts-20261024">TNPSC Group IV recruitment for 6,574 posts</a> and <a href="https://growentix.cloud/articles/tntet-working-teachers-2026-20261027">Tamil Nadu Teacher Eligibility Test for working teachers</a>. The TNPSC reporting in this article is drawn from <a href="https://menafn.com/1111876725/Nearly-49-Lakh-Candidates-To-Appear-For-TNPSC-Group-II-IIA-Prelims-On-November-1">the IANS report carried by MENAFN</a> and <a href="https://www.dtnext.in/amp/story/news/tamilnadu/tnpsc-group-ii-iia-hall-tickets-released">the DT Next report on hall tickets</a>, with the official websites given as tnpsc.gov.in and tnpscexams.in.</p>$$,
